  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Composition and ownership",
      "items": [
        "Descriptor and Dynamics are selected in C++; inputs expose case controls.",
        "Showcases currently use single-level grids. Multilevel cases live in tests/numerical.",
        "AMR performance code reuses numerical compositions, without their scientific acceptance policy.",
        "Observers are supplied by each case; they consume published states and own sampling/output decisions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Evolution and shared implementation",
      "items": [
        "Both paths use collision, streaming, field storage and boundary reconstruction.",
        "LbmAmrSimulation owns its hierarchy and fields; adjacent levels use CoarseFineCoupling.",
        "Fine levels subcycle twice. Static layouts do not regrid; dynamic layouts regrid only at the configured synchronized interval.",
        "Regridding remaps populations and rebuilds affected coupling/boundary state. Interval forces are collected before regridding."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "How to read this overview",
      "items": [
        "AI-generated with Archify from inspected source. Arrows show calls or dependencies, not one universal execution sequence.",
        "Groups summarize cooperating classes, not separate processes. This is a navigation overview, not an exhaustive class diagram.",
        "AMReX is installed separately and linked through find_package; CPU and CUDA builds use distinct packages.",
        "Dynamic AMR is experimental. CPU MPI was tested; resource-limited GPU parallel tests covered MIG slices, not multiple full GPUs. See README limitations."
      ]
    }
  ]
